content.text:
Confirmed working — the new run at `11:57` pulled the credentials from config (`AdminUser: rtsadmin`, `AdminPassword: adminrts@#12345@#rtsadmin` for the `"Com"` entry) and produced identical results: ERI00139 (Sri Annai) and ERI00517 (Lizma) both populated correctly, Excel saved to `OCR_AI_Report_20260724_1157.xlsx`.

**What changed:** `server_ip_list` entries in `appsettings.json` now carry their own `Ip`, `AdminUser`, and `AdminPassword` instead of the app hardcoding one login for everyone. To switch environments, you just edit config — no code change needed:

```json
"Com": { "Ip": "103.211.202.235", "AdminUser": "rtsadmin", "AdminPassword": "adminrts@#12345@#rtsadmin" }
```
```json
//"Local": { "Ip": "SERVER\\SQLEXPRESS", "AdminUser": "sa", "AdminPassword": "SA,123" }
```

Uncomment `"Local"` (and comment out `"Com"`) to test against your local SQLEXPRESS with `sa`, or add more live servers each with their own creds — the app will automatically use whichever credentials belong to whichever server entries are active, and can even run multiple servers in one pass since it loops through all uncommented entries.

That's by design, not a bug — every test run we've done used the `--dry-run` flag, and the code explicitly skips the email step when that flag is present:

```csharp
if (isDryRun)
{
    Logger.Info("Dry run: skipping email send. Report left at {0} for inspection.", savePath);
    return;
}

await SendEmailWithAttachment(savePath);
```

That's exactly the safeguard we added earlier so testing wouldn't spam real inboxes every time you run it (`Program.cs`, look for `isDryRun`).

To actually send the email, just run it **without** the flag:
```
dotnet run
```
That will go through the same DB pull, generate the Excel, and this time also call `SendEmailWithAttachment` and send it to `clientstatus@royaltechsystems.co.in` (CC: shrikaanth, karthickraja, jayasris, srimansubash) — then delete the local file afterward, same as the very first run.

Since that's a real send to real people, want me to run it now, or would you rather trigger it yourself?

